    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n17668" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">fămŭlāris</span></b> ,
		e, adj.
		famulus, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>of</i> or <i>belonging to
			servants</i> or <i>slaves</i> (rare but class.):
			vestis, * <i><span class="en">Cic. Tusc. 1, 48, 116</span></i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">turba,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Stat.
					Ach. 2, 67</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">jugum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sen. Troad. 747</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">jura,</span>”
				i. e. <i>of subjugation</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Ov.
					M. 15, 597</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">hederae,</span>”
				<i>the Bacchantes</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Val.
					Fl. 2, 268</span></i>
			.—In the <i>neutr.</i> adverb., <i>servilely</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nec famulare timens,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Stat.
					S. 3, 1, 40</span></i>
			.</div>
